{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath .\\\\skill\\\\references\\\\presentation-checks.md -Encoding utf8 -Raw; Get-Content -LiteralPath .\\\\skill\\\\references\\\\gameplay-coherence.md -Encoding utf8 -Raw; Get-Content -LiteralPath .\\\\skill\\\\references\\\\runtime-checks.md -Encoding utf8 -Raw; Get-Content -LiteralPath .\\\\skill\\\\references\\\\skill-handoff.md -Encoding utf8 -Raw\"","aggregated_output":"# Evidence and presentation checks\r\n\r\nUse the cases relevant to the requested flow. These are hypotheses and illustrative examples, not measured thresholds or a list to clear in every game. For interacting actions, transitions or motion/logic alignment, use [gameplay-coherence.md](gameplay-coherence.md). A visible expression mismatch against established intent can be a defect even when individual functions work.\r\n\r\n## From symptom to a justified change\r\n\r\n| Candidate symptom | Evidence needed | Small correction | Reason to keep it |\r\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r\n| Mixed pixel or icon density | Compare assets at their intended display scale and filtering; inspect import settings before attributing the cause | Match the outlier's scale or sampling to its peers | Mixed resolution may deliberately separate UI from world art |\r\n| Font that looks like an engine default | Show a mismatch with the game's established typography, missing glyphs or unreadable text | Reuse a suitable existing type style while retaining language coverage | A default font can be an intentional, readable choice |\r\n| Bloom around text | Compare the same label over representative bright and dark backgrounds in the runtime | Adjust the source of the glow that obscures the label | Emissive signage may be part of navigation or identity |\r\n| Celebration for every ordinary pickup | Observe repeated pickups and whether the cue blocks input or masks a higher-priority event | Shorten, combine or lower the repeated cue; retain an accurate receipt | A milestone or rhythm-game action may warrant strong feedback |\r\n| Progress without a matching state change | Trace what the indicator claims and which actual operation drives it | Show the confirmed state or label an estimate/indeterminate wait honestly | Background work can legitimately progress without player input |\r\n| Particles cover a telegraph | Observe a dense encounter and check whether the attack cue remains recognizable | Reduce the conflicting layer or repetition | Raw particle count alone says nothing about visibility or cost |\r\n| Recurring screen shake | Observe its trigger, duration, stacking and reduced-motion path | Bound or adjust the distracting camera effect | Do not change gameplay hit-stop or timing windows as a cosmetic fix |\r\n| Repeated sounds hide an alert | Listen to the relevant events together under the game's mix settings | Reuse category controls or reduce repeated cues | Audio can be the primary accessible information channel |\r\n| Deep UI hierarchy | Identify actual clipping, incorrect focus, scaling problems or measured layout cost | Fix the responsible container or input route | Nesting depth is not a defect by itself |\r\n| “Tap to continue” or enthusiastic copy | Check input method, context and whether the instruction is accurate | Correct a wrong input label or replace hype that hides the reward | A clear touch prompt or authored character voice needs no rewrite |\r\n\r\nSearch relevant text resources with existing repository tools to locate candidates. Search matches do not establish player-visible defects. A dedicated scanner is justified only by repeated work on a known format with examples of true and false positives; do not create one merely to assign a slop score.\r\n\r\n## Capture, inspect, verify\r\n\r\n1. **Record the conditions.** Identify scene/state, viewport or device, UI scale, language, input method and accessibility mode when known. Mark missing facts instead of inferring them from a cropped image. Keep the before capture if available.\r\n2. **Describe observable interference.** Name the element and what it overlaps, competes with or misrepresents. Connect that evidence to a player action. Separate visible symptoms from source-code hypotheses; visual prominence alone is not a defect.\r\n3. **Revisit the same conditions.** Compare before/after in the same state, then check the relevant stress case: long text, a dense encounter, safe-area limits, controller focus or repeated notifications. State which conditions were actually exercised.\r\n\r\nA still capture supports static layout observations only. Use a sequence or runtime for transitions and repeated motion, listening for audio claims, and source/state evidence for networking claims. Do not claim frame rate, latency, texel density or a contrast ratio from appearance alone. Measure when the task and tools support it; use the project's platform targets rather than invented universal numbers. Never claim an accessibility audit passed from a screenshot alone.\r\n\r\n## UI usability checks\r\n\r\nSelect checks relevant to the scene and input. Familiar appearance, minimalism and compliance with a named psychology law are not verdicts. Connect each concern to a task, evidence, preservation needs and a discriminating check.\r\n\r\n- Placement and hierarchy: inspect whether required information competes with decoration, obscures play, or requires avoidable search. A screenshot can establish captured occlusion, not a player's search time. Preserve deliberate off-center or dense layouts when they support the task. Check finding the next action at the target size.\r\n- Shape and hit regions: compare interactive and decorative elements, actual hit geometry, target spacing and focus/press states. Separate a demonstrated overlap or blocked target from an untested prediction of mistaps. Preserve distinctive shapes and useful icon-plus-text labels. Check activation, cancellation and neighboring targets with the relevant input.\r\n- Navigation and grouping: inspect consistent meanings, focus movement, back/close paths and the cost of hiding related information. Fewer visible options are not automatically better. Check the complete requested task and return path; preserve necessary expert information and accessibility cues.\r\n- Contrast and identity: inspect relevant backgrounds and actual display scale. Compare outliers with documented or provisional authored references. Do not mandate a backplate, shadow, font replacement, material or genre palette. If a visual alternative lacks a demonstrated cost, label it optional and compare its gains and losses.\r\n- Device and evidence limits: record viewport, UI scale, input, language and accessibility settings. Mobile emulation can check layout and event routing, not prove thumb reach or comfort. Without runtime or user evidence, keep claims about repeated errors, reaction time and comprehension unverified.\r\n\r\nRepair the smallest supported problem while preserving accurate state, control availability, non-color signals and identity. For requested UI construction or broader layout/style alternatives, explain the relevant bettermakegame scope without requiring its installation or an automatic second pass. Do not drop independently supported local repairs merely because that skill is unavailable.\r\n\r\n## Inspect relevant moments\r\n\r\n1. Select conditions from the requested flow: a relevant notice appearing or clearing, reachable simultaneous events, and an affected element near a viewport or panel boundary. Reuse existing captures and fixtures. Do not enumerate every event pair or expand into unrelated screens.\r\n2. Reproduce the condition with recorded starting state and triggers. Label scripted input or injected state and establish reachability before calling the condition a normal-play failure. Mutually exclusive normal branches do not rule out stale callbacks or re-entry; leave those paths unverified until inspected. Use a timed sequence for duration claims; mark unavailable observations explicitly.\r\n3. Inspect visibility, state meaning and input routing. When bounds or draw-order data already exist, use them to locate candidates. Rectangle intersection alone is not a defect: a label may be inside its own panel, the panel may be transparent, or drawing may be clipped. Unknown or tied ordering requires inspection, not an automatic pass or failure.\r\n4. Connect an observed symptom to the affected decision or action. An AI reader's uncertain interpretation is a hypothesis to investigate, not a measured human error. Preserve labels and redundant channels that serve a purpose. Choose the smallest supported correction, with the unchanged version remaining valid when no cost is demonstrated.\r\n5. Recheck the changed condition and directly affected neighbors. Keep the existing finding format; attach the moment and evidence rather than creating a separate score or report. State which checks were not run.\r\n\r\n## Finding format\r\n\r\nUse one record per decision, scaled to the evidence. Do not create separate reports for each field or invent findings to fill a template.\r\n\r\n| Field | Record |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| ID / scene / judgment | Stable ID, state, and confirmed defect / hypothesis / optional proposal / preserve |\r\n| Evidence / provenance | Screenshot path + frame; video + time interval; runtime + steps; source + symbol/revision; or attributed user report. Include a moment ID and reproduction path when relevant. List multiple sources separately |\r\n| Observed scope / unknowns | Viewport, language, input, accessibility mode, relevant state and exactly what was observed; normal play, scripted input or state injection; missing conditions, uncertain causes and unverified reachability |\r\n| Player impact / expression mismatch | Action/information affected, or the observed discontinuity and intended relationship it violates. Separate observation from inferred consequences; no fabricated human error, enjoyment or retention claim. For proposals, state expected benefit |\r\n| Correction / disposition | Proposed or applied smallest change, or preserved/deferred/rejected with reason |\r\n| Preserve / tradeoff | Identity, accessible channels, state truth, timing and meaningful effects that must remain; expected losses if a proposal is adopted |\r\n| Success condition | Observable task outcome under specified conditions, plus the preservation check; name the next check if evidence is missing |\r\n| Verification | Not run / blocked / passed / failed / mixed, actual method and conditions, artifact links, and remaining limits. Applied is not verified |\r\n\r\nEvidence limits:\r\n\r\n- **Collection:** select the relevant UI region, DOM elements and log fields; avoid whole-page text, storage dumps and unrelated chat/account data. Mask or omit sensitive material before saving or passing evidence onward. Use fresh run paths and preserve original before/after artifacts; a redacted derivative is a separate artifact. Record that redaction occurred without reproducing the removed value.\r\n\r\n- **Screenshot:** establishes the captured layout; it cannot establish repeated interruption, animation timing, sound, input behavior or server acceptance.\r\n- **Video:** establishes the recorded interval, with audible evidence only when the track was actually available and inspected. Do not infer all sessions from one interval.\r\n- **Runtime:** state the steps/environment you actually exercised; synthetic events, test instrumentation and mocks must be disclosed.\r\n- **Source:** can establish a code path or a violated contract with sufficient context. It does not establish a rendered defect, timing in production or that the path ran for the user.\r\n- **User report:** preserve the attribution and reported scope. Do not relabel it as your own reproduction. Lack of reproduction does not disprove the report.\r\n\r\nExample record (illustrative): `F01 / combat / confirmed static overlap`: supplied `fight.png` at 1280×720 shows a reward banner over the wind-up marker. Repetition is separately user-reported; animation and other resolutions are unverified. Player may lose the cue needed to dodge. Propose moving the banner outside that region; preserve reward count and milestone emphasis. Success: both cue and count visible during the same encounter and repeated pickups. Verification: not run; runtime required for the repeated sequence.\r\n\r\nPrioritize by consequence and recurrence: false hit/state cues and lost control can matter more than a brief pose discontinuity; a pervasive expression defect is not automatically trivial because input still works. If no supported functional or expression mismatch is found, say **no confirmed problems in the reviewed conditions**, preserve the element and list material uncertainties. An optional proposal does not become a failed QA check merely because it was not adopted.\r\n\r\n## Real application record\r\n\r\nKeep the intent, before capture and conditions, decisions (including preserved elements), adopted/rejected changes, comparable after capture, and success-check results together. For an unchanged scene, explicitly record a preservation outcome and why no patch was justified; do not manufacture a modification to complete a case study. Clearly label historical captures, synthetic fixtures and current live captures. When collecting human feedback, record task, order, sample and observations separately from automated assertions.\r\n\r\n# Gameplay coherence\n\nRead for awkward action combinations, motion/logic discrepancies, transitions or repeated/contextual feedback. Apply only relevant checks. A working movement function plus a working attack function does not establish that their combination works. The same principle applies to climbing while interacting, vehicle boarding, dialogue choices, inventory use and reward flows. This is not a requirement to audit every system.\n\n## Establish a small behavior contract\n\nUse the existing request, design, authored references and inspected implementation. Record only what affects the finding, inline when sufficient:\n\n> Which actions may coexist? What changes movement, rotation or control ownership? When do meaningful events occur? What survives cancellation, interruption or exit? What expression must remain?\n\nLabel design facts, implementation facts and assumptions separately. Existing code demonstrates implementation, not necessarily intent. If no authoritative intent is available, compare nearby established behavior and state the inference. Ask only when choosing between materially different rules; do not invent an attack lock, cancel window or frame count.\n\nMoving attacks can be correct. A rooted heavy attack, a moving upper-body attack and an authored lunge need different contracts. Gameplay state, animation state and control ownership need not be one-to-one, nor fit one exclusive state enum. Layered animation, procedural motion, sprite changes and separate locomotion/action state can all be valid. Do not prescribe architecture merely to simplify a review.\n\n## Inspect the connections\n\n| Connection | Inspect | Counterexample to preserve |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Combined actions | Movement/rotation policy, pose ownership, input routing and animation channels while actions overlap | Lower-body locomotion plus upper-body attack; intentional strafe or cast movement |\n| Entry, interruption and recovery | Entry pose, meaningful action phases, cancel/hit/death, control return and stale callbacks | An intentional commitment window or recoverable stagger; short transitions chosen for responsive controls |\n| Space and contact | Visible body/weapon, actual root/collider, contact points, travel direction and relevant terrain/platform motion in the same coordinates | Deliberate hovering/skating, exaggerated reach, abstract collision; planted feet on moving platforms need a surface-relative comparison |\n| Time and causality | Input receipt, acceptance, anticipation, hit/miss/block, sound/effect and settled state on the actual clocks | Intentional anticipation, delayed blast, travel time, hit-stop or provisional network feedback with truthful meaning |\n| State and affordance | What the player is told they can do versus accepted/rejected input and its explanation | Rules that intentionally restrict an action while communicating why |\n| Context and identity | Cue, text, asset and actual event agree; meaningful visual/audio vocabulary persists | Deliberate mixed media, comic exaggeration, stylized pose snapping or a different milestone vocabulary |\n| Repetition and closure | Reachable repeated triggers, cue stacking, return to play, skip/cancel handling and accurate final result | Repetition that is the core loop, an important celebration, or accessible redundant channels |\n\nThese are candidate checks, not automatic defects. Sparse animation, common assets, a familiar genre or repeated content alone does not demonstrate slop. Content with no apparent purpose may warrant a hypothesis and task-based review; do not infer boredom, retention or a need for new systems. Low frame rate, network interpolation, asset mismatch and blend configuration are possible causes of a symptom, not diagnoses from appearance alone.\n\n## Follow a meaningful sequence\n\nObserve the requested action alone, the relevant combination, and the important interruption/return path. For walking plus attack, select from: begin attacking while moving; begin moving mid-attack; release/reverse input; repeat attack; cancel or receive a hit; resume or replace the owner. Include only reachable and material variants, not a Cartesian product of all states.\n\nCompare the same input, character, camera, speed, equipment and settings before/after. Record timestamps or frames when available. Normal-speed playback establishes how the sequence presents; slow motion or frame stepping helps locate a discontinuity. Frame stepping alone does not establish perceived severity. A screenshot cannot prove foot sliding or timing. Logs can confirm event order, but scheduled sound is not evidence that the sound was heard.\n\nWhen tools allow, correlate observed motion with velocity, facing, action ID/phase, animation layer/weight, visible-root/collider transforms and hit/cancel events. Collect only signals needed to distinguish causes. State coordinate space, clock and injected versus ordinary input. Account for moving ground, interpolation and camera motion before calling a foot or mesh displaced. Do not create universal pixel, degree or millisecond tolerances; use the project's contract, sampling precision and visible evidence.\n\n## Diagnose and repair at the responsible layer\n\nSeparate three questions: **what was observed**, **what intended relationship it breaks**, and **what caused it**. For example, “during the supplied moving attack, the documented planted foot translates relative to stationary ground” can establish an expression mismatch without proving a blend-tree cause. A missing mask, speed mismatch, conflicting transform writer or unsuitable clip remains a hypothesis until traced. An observed presentation defect does not require a measured input failure or human preference study, but its unintended nature needs a basis.\n\nPrefer the smallest repair at the owning layer: animation composition or asset adaptation for a pose mismatch; transform/coordinate ownership for a body/collider split; lifecycle handling for a stale cue. Keep movement permission, hit windows, authority and intentional motion intact. Root Motion, a new clip or an animation layer may be appropriate, but none is mandatory. Do not change combat balance or use camera shake, particles or forced immobility to conceal the mismatch.\n\nCancellation policy is action-specific. Interrupting an attack may cancel its pending contact, while a projectile already released may legitimately survive the actor. Record which effect or event belongs to which lifetime; do not delete every effect on interruption. A superseded animation callback must not release a replacement action's control lock or emit its hit. On pause, use the game's actual clock policy rather than assuming every timer freezes.\n\nAfter the final edit, replay the implicated sequence and affected shared users. Functional invariants and rendered expression are separate checks. Keep visual/audio/device checks unverified if the relevant tools are unavailable; do useful source work without inventing a viewed result.\n\n## Report through the existing finding\n\nUse the [finding record](presentation-checks.md#finding-format). Identify the relationship and rule/reference basis in the scene/evidence fields; describe functional impact or an observed expression mismatch in the impact field. Keep judgment (defect, hypothesis, optional proposal, preserve) separate from problem area and priority. An observation can be confirmed while its cause is unknown. A style alternative remains optional; “slop” is not a severity label or percentage.\n\nFor identity trials, include the unchanged version and expected losses. For a defect, success must describe what now aligns and what remains unchanged. Do not mark the whole scene coherent from a single successful trace or attribute bugs to AI authorship.\n\n## Engine mechanisms, not required solutions\n\n- [Unity Animation Layers](https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/AnimationLayers.html) describes lower-body locomotion with upper-body actions and masks. This supports valid simultaneous actions, not a mandate to split every attack.\n- [Unreal Root Motion](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/root-motion-in-unreal-engine) illustrates visible mesh displacement and character movement ownership. Use the actual project's configuration and version; an unseen project is not diagnosed by that example.\n\nThese references explain mechanisms. They do not certify this skill or any native engine integration.\n\r\n# Runtime and human checks\n\nRead for the actual renderer, input or playback checks needed by a repair.\n\nUse an isolated scene and a fresh test session for interaction checks. Inspect unfamiliar launch/build scripts and their hooks before execution. If a check would change live payments, public content, rewards or user data beyond existing authorization, use a local substitute or leave that check pending while continuing feasible work.\n\nFor browser checks, use a new context with Service Workers blocked, exact scheme/host/port request allowlists and an explicit WebSocket policy. Apply routing before creating pages, including popups. Inspect redirect handling as well as direct requests. Log denied origins without URL credentials, paths, query strings or bodies. Browser routing is a limited traffic policy, not Node/process or host network isolation; untrusted executable projects require host isolation too. Do not promise complete containment from these browser settings.\n\nCapture only the scene and state fields needed for the finding. Use dummy identities; omit or mask account/chat overlays, credentials and unrelated logs before writing artifacts. Recheck saved evidence for the dummy values when validating a collector. Never overwrite baseline captures or previous results; create a distinct run directory and record actual source hashes and tool/browser versions.\n\nEngine-specific checks are conditional, not mandatory dependencies:\n\n- Unity: inspect prefab overrides, serialized references, Canvas layout and the project's actual UI/input stack.\n- Unreal: inspect widget/Blueprint references, focus/navigation, anchors and safe zones with available editor tools.\n- Godot: inspect Control containers, size flags, theme overrides, signals and the project's input actions.\n- Roblox: inspect full client initialization, GUI bounds and remotes; compile Luau at O0 as well as the project's normal optimization when a compiler is available.\n- Web/custom engines: inspect DOM or canvas scaling, device pixels, focus/input routing and scene cleanup in the actual runtime.\n\nFor action/motion findings, follow [gameplay coherence](gameplay-coherence.md) and inspect the actual controller/animation/physics boundary: Unity Animator layers/masks and movement ownership; Unreal Anim Blueprint/Montage and movement component; Godot AnimationTree/AnimationPlayer and body motion; Roblox animation priority and character movement; or the custom renderer's event/transform owners. These are inspection routes, not required APIs or certified engine adapters. Compare visible motion with relevant state traces; a passing state test does not validate animation quality, and a rendered pose does not prove the authoritative hit result.\n\nFor human checks, give a concrete task (identify the selected piece, find the next action, recognize an attack cue), not only “Is this better?”. Where feasible, alternate before/after order or use different first viewers to reduce learning effects. Record observations and their limited sample; automated checks establish behavior, not human comprehension or fun.\n\r\n# Scoped design and VFX handoff\n\nRead only when a review leads to requested interaction/design work or effect production with another skill. A local repair can stay here; neither a finding nor a companion's availability requires a second audit, installation, agent, or separate report.\n\n## Pass the decision, not an inflated diagnosis\n\nReuse the existing finding ID and short game-intent record. Pass only:\n\n> Player task and requested scope; judgment and evidence/provenance; observed condition and unknowns; smallest requested change; what must remain; observable success condition.\n\nFor broader requested UI/control design, `bettermakegame` can compare techniques and implement within scope. For an actual VFX asset/integration task, `game-vfx` can render and test the effect. Resolve companions through the host's available skills, never a fixed neighbor directory or a supposed executable with the skill's name. Continue feasible work alone if a companion is unavailable.\n\nCarry **review-only** scope unchanged: proposed design or effect work stays a proposal. An optional style alternative cannot become a required bug fix in transit. Preserve evidence limits: an attributed report is not a reproduction, a source finding is not a viewed render, and a still cannot prove sound masking or repeated distraction.\n\nFor action coherence, carry the allowed combination, movement/control ownership, relevant event timing and cancellation policy. Distinguish a confirmed visual mismatch from a suspected controller/animation cause. A producer must not stop permitted movement or shift damage timing to make an asset appear correct. A rule change needs its own design basis and authorization; repair of an established-rule violation is assessed against that rule.\n\nExample, not a reported game defect:\n\n> F07 / confirmed source-state mismatch in the inspected handler: a local pending request triggers the success cue before server acknowledgement. Repair that cue path only. Preserve the authored bright impact style, actual collision/turn rules and input acknowledgement. Check pending, confirmed, denied, duplicate and previous-round responses. Rendering and sound are still unverified.\n\nDo not turn this example into a rule that all optimistic feedback is wrong. Clearly provisional feedback can be useful; it must not misrepresent a confirmed result.\n\n## Consume the returned evidence\n\nReuse the producer's files, parameters, before/after conditions and actual checks in the original finding. Keep **applied**, **verified**, **remaining discrepancy** and **approved departure** distinct. A producer's “done” message or screenshot count does not close the finding. Recheck the success and preservation conditions affected by the final edit; do not restart unrelated analysis.\n\nFor a pending/online cue, inspect event identity, round/owner lifetime and actual state order. Test late, duplicate, denied and corrected messages where reachable, including a higher revision after the effect has finished. Keep that presentation lifetime closed unless the actual protocol explicitly creates a new one; an old callback must not finish a reused instance. A wrong success cue is a state problem; reduce particles only if they independently obscure information. Do not alter collision, network authority or simulation clocks to repair feedback.\n\nFor audio/VFX overlap, report which channels were actually experienced. Source routing or scheduled playback can support a causal hypothesis; hearing and motion claims need the relevant playback evidence. A headless command log cannot establish mix quality or haptic feel.\n\nIf the result meets the scoped condition, close that finding with its evidence and limits. If a material mismatch remains, request one targeted revision tied to that condition. Do not bounce indefinitely between skills or broaden the task to justify more changes. Purposeful dense effects and an unchanged scene remain valid outcomes.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
